 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Cropping is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of points. Voxel downsampling is $O(N)$ with a hash map lookup. Uniform downsampling is $O(N)$ for sampling if using reservoir sampling or $O(N \log N)$ if sorting/shuffling. ICP registration is typically $O(M \log M)$ or $O(M)$ depending on the nearest neighbor search, where $M$ is the downsampled point count.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the point cloud. Voxel downsampling requires $O(K)$ memory for the voxel grid, where $K$ is the number of occupied voxels.
-- **Strengths:** Efficiently reduces point cloud size while preserving spatial distribution (voxel) or random representation (uniform). Integrates seamlessly with ICP registration pipelines [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Voxel downsampling may lose fine details if `voxel_size` is too large. Uniform downsampling may remove important features if they are sparse.
-- **When to Use:** Use voxel downsampling when preserving spatial density is important for registration or voxelization. Use uniform downsampling when a random subset is sufficient or when computational simplicity is preferred.
+- **Time Complexity**: Cropping is $O(N)$. Voxel downsampling is $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of cropped points, assuming hash map operations are $O(1)$. Uniform downsampling is $O(M)$ for selection.
+- **Memory Complexity**: $O(M)$ for storing cropped points. Voxel grid storage is proportional to the number of occupied voxels.
+- **Strengths**: Simple to implement; voxel downsampling provides a regularized point distribution suitable for grid-based methods; uniform downsampling is fast and preserves global distribution if density is uniform.
+- **Limitations**: Voxel downsampling may lose fine details if `voxel_size` is too large; uniform downsampling may cluster points in dense regions; cropping requires accurate bounding box definition.
+- **When to use**: Preprocessing for voxelization, registration, or rendering where point count reduction is needed.
+- **When not to use**: When preserving exact geometric fidelity is critical and downsampling artifacts are unacceptable; when point density varies significantly and uniform/voxel methods would bias the result.
 
 ## Background
-The method is part of the preprocessing pipeline for 3D point cloud processing, particularly in the context of 2D Gaussian Splatting and ICP registration [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in the `2d-gaussian-splatting` repository [S1, unofficial]. The use of point clouds as a fundamental primitive for 3D representation has a long history in computer graphics [S2].
+Originating from standard point cloud processing pipelines, this method is commonly used in 3D reconstruction and registration tasks [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in libraries such as Open3D and used in Gaussian Splatting pipelines for efficient rendering and training [S1, unofficial][S3]. The method addresses the computational bottleneck of processing large point clouds by reducing data volume while maintaining essential geometric information.
 
